WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.360
Two gray squirrels released in 1876 replaced red squirrels across English lowland woods. This

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:12.640
is unintended consequences, episode 173. In 1876, Victorian banker Thomas V. Brockelhurst

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.960
released a pair of Eastern gray squirrels he had brought from New York into Henry Park

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:23.000
near Mackelsfield, Cheshire. The animals escaped and multiplied. They carried a parapox virus

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.920
harmless to themselves, but usually fatal to native red squirrels, and they outcompeted

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.000
those red squirrels for food and habitat.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.960
Within decades, red squirrel populations had collapsed across southern England and the

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:35.380
Midlands.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:41.320
The decision to add one attractive species to a private estate set off two pressures, disease

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:45.280
and resource competition, that no one at the time measured or modeled.

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:48.960
What followed was not a gradual mixing of squirrel populations.

00:00:50.036 --> 00:00:53.156
Thomas V. Brocklehurst had traveled to America on business.

00:00:53.636 --> 00:00:57.916
He brought the pair home because he found the animals attractive as pets once their novelty

00:00:57.916 --> 00:01:03.776
wore off on his estate. Other landowners followed the same impulse. The eleventh duke of Bedford,

00:01:04.076 --> 00:01:09.016
her brand Russell, kept gray squirrels at Woolburn Park and gave animals to other estates,

00:01:09.216 --> 00:01:13.676
including Regions Park in London. The state owners viewed the introductions as harmless

00:01:13.676 --> 00:01:18.596
enhancements to private parks and gardens, much like the release of other ornamental species.

00:01:19.196 --> 00:01:23.876
No one had yet documented the virus transmission or the scale of competition that would follow.

00:01:23.876 --> 00:01:29.376
The releases sat inside of Victorian enthusiasm for collecting and displaying exotic wildlife

00:01:29.376 --> 00:01:30.536
on country estates.

00:01:30.996 --> 00:01:34.976
The state records show that owners tracked the visible presence of the new squirrels on

00:01:34.976 --> 00:01:39.776
their grounds, but kept no systematic counts of native red squirrels already living there.

00:01:40.316 --> 00:01:44.816
Because the gray squirrels appeared healthy and bred readily in captivity, the assumption

00:01:44.816 --> 00:01:49.516
was that they would simply add color and activity without displacing anything already present.

00:01:49.516 --> 00:01:55.968
Monika Shorten and Christopher Leaver later listed the introductions that followed the 1876 release.

00:01:56.608 --> 00:02:00.588
Ten squirrels from New Jersey stock were released at Woolburn Park in 1890.

00:02:01.348 --> 00:02:04.368
100 animals went into Richmond Park in 1902.

00:02:05.008 --> 00:02:09.548
91 were released in Regions Park between 1905 and 1907.

00:02:10.248 --> 00:02:16.448
Additional releases occurred at sites in Berkshire, Staffordshire, Devon and Hampshire through 1929,

00:02:16.868 --> 00:02:19.128
when the last recorded introduction took place.

00:02:19.128 --> 00:02:23.068
Proponents at the time simply noted the squirrel's visibility in parks.

00:02:23.688 --> 00:02:25.508
No coordinated monitoring existed.

00:02:26.128 --> 00:02:30.508
Even the smallest founding groups proved sufficient, because gray squirrels reached breeding

00:02:30.508 --> 00:02:34.608
age in their first year and can produce two litters annually when food is plentiful.

00:02:35.308 --> 00:02:39.308
Landowners continued the practice because each new sighting was treated as confirmation

00:02:39.308 --> 00:02:43.768
that the animals were settling in successfully, rather than as a signal to stop.

00:02:44.448 --> 00:02:49.828
Repeated small gifts from woolburn and similar states created multiple independent nuclei rather

00:02:49.828 --> 00:02:51.188
than one expanding front.

00:02:52.225 --> 00:02:56.965
Gray squirrels expanded from these centers, aided by further human translocations that DNA

00:02:56.965 --> 00:03:00.785
profiling of nearly 1,500 animals later traced.

00:03:01.305 --> 00:03:07.485
A 2000 study in animal conservation tested 223 apparently healthy gray squirrels and found

00:03:07.485 --> 00:03:10.805
61% carried antibodies to the parapox virus.

00:03:10.805 --> 00:03:17.785
Only 2.86% of 140 red squirrels showed exposure, and three of those displayed active disease.

00:03:18.345 --> 00:03:22.225
The Forestry Commission later recorded that gray squirrels stripped bark from trees,

00:03:22.745 --> 00:03:27.785
reducing timber quality and imposing an estimated 37 million pounds in annual cost.

00:03:28.385 --> 00:03:36.345
Red squirrels disappeared from 70 of 73 surveyed forests by 1935, and from 19 of 77 by 1955,

00:03:36.345 --> 00:03:41.665
In districts examined by Southeastern Association of Fish and Wildlife Agency's researchers.

00:03:42.165 --> 00:03:47.165
The virus acted as a reservoir maintained in the gray population, while direct competition

00:03:47.165 --> 00:03:52.085
for food and habitat accelerated the retreat of red squirrels to fragmented northern and island

00:03:52.085 --> 00:03:52.785
refugia.

00:03:53.365 --> 00:03:58.725
Human-assisted movements, documented by Imperial College London researchers, created genetically

00:03:58.725 --> 00:04:03.825
distinct populations far from natural dispersal range, including the Aberdeen group linked

00:04:03.825 --> 00:04:04.745
to Hampshire stock.

00:04:04.745 --> 00:04:10.565
The Parapox virus spreads when grays and reds share feeding sites, or when reds investigate

00:04:10.565 --> 00:04:12.585
grays previously used by grays.

00:04:13.125 --> 00:04:16.585
Reds develop skin lesions and systemic illness that grays do not.

00:04:17.245 --> 00:04:22.085
At the same time, gray squirrels can digest acorns and other hardmast more efficiently and

00:04:22.085 --> 00:04:25.405
cache larger stores, leaving less for reds during winter.

00:04:25.885 --> 00:04:30.265
Because the two pressures operated together, even woodlands that retained some tree cover

00:04:30.265 --> 00:04:34.905
saw red numbers fall below viable levels within 20 to 30 years of gray arrival.

00:04:35.385 --> 00:04:40.185
The DNA evidence further shows that natural dispersal alone could not account for the speed

00:04:40.185 --> 00:04:41.305
or pattern of spread.

00:04:41.925 --> 00:04:46.065
Repeated human gifts multiplied the number of starting points and therefore the total

00:04:46.065 --> 00:04:47.125
area under pressure.

00:04:48.111 --> 00:04:51.551
A 1937 prohibition order ended deliberate releases.

00:04:52.191 --> 00:04:57.191
Modern management focuses on targeted gray squirrel control in priority red squirrel areas.

00:04:57.191 --> 00:05:02.531
The Forestry Commission reports that this work has allowed local red populations to stabilize,

00:05:02.971 --> 00:05:08.671
and the same control work also reduces tree damage. Red squirrels remain protected and are now

00:05:08.671 --> 00:05:14.431
found mainly in northern England, Scotland, Ireland, and a few southern islands. No reversal of the

00:05:14.431 --> 00:05:20.251
overall range shift has occurred. Control programs must be sustained year after year because any

00:05:20.251 --> 00:05:25.951
relaxation allows grey squirrels to re-enter from surrounding land. The economic case for continued

00:05:25.951 --> 00:05:31.871
action now rests on both biodiversity targets and that annual timber loss, so the original

00:05:31.871 --> 00:05:36.011
ornamental impulse still generates measurable costs more than a century later.

00:05:36.959 --> 00:05:41.619
When estate owners chose to move small numbers of gray squirrels onto new ground, they

00:05:41.619 --> 00:05:46.419
rewarded the animal's ability to exploit both novel food sources and an existing disease

00:05:46.419 --> 00:05:48.359
vulnerability in the resident species.

00:05:48.919 --> 00:05:53.779
When later managers tracked sarrow prevalence rather than visible symptoms alone, they

00:05:53.779 --> 00:05:58.019
They identified the reservoir mechanism that single species introductions had created.

00:05:58.499 --> 00:06:03.659
When DNA worked distinguished human-transported populations from natural spread, it showed

00:06:03.659 --> 00:06:08.299
that repeated small gifts from woolburn and similar sites had multiplied the outcome far

00:06:08.299 --> 00:06:09.699
beyond the original releases.

00:06:10.339 --> 00:06:14.859
Each of these steps reveals that the critical variable was not the size of any single release,

00:06:15.419 --> 00:06:19.939
but the cumulative effect of many independent decisions made without reference to pathogen

00:06:19.939 --> 00:06:21.799
carriage or competitive displacement.

00:06:21.799 --> 00:06:26.679
environment. Environmental intelligence keeps returning to this kind of ledger, where a private

00:06:26.679 --> 00:06:31.379
choice about living things becomes a public cost, measured in forests, and in species that

00:06:31.379 --> 00:06:36.399
cannot simply be put back. How might current decisions about moving or tolerating any

00:06:36.399 --> 00:06:41.279
one species account for hidden pathogen and competition effects before the next translocation

00:06:41.279 --> 00:06:46.399
occurs? That's the case for today. The lessons tend to travel further when you share them,

00:06:46.779 --> 00:06:50.999
so send this one to someone who'd find it useful, and subscribe wherever you're listening.

